        "The 2022 reform that ended most of Mexico's daylight-saving changes is genuinely useful for scheduling: a family in Mexico City, Monterrey, Guadalajara or Puebla can fix an evening slot once and expect it to hold for the whole school year, unlike countries where clocks still jump twice a year. It's not universal, though — the northern border strip stayed aligned with US clock changes for trade reasons, and Quintana Roo has kept its own fixed UTC-5 since 2015 — so a family in Tijuana or Cancún should mention that up front rather than assume the Mexico City default applies to them.",
